This add-on assumes the pardiso package to be available and installed.
The package is part of MKL or can be obtained for various architectures from 
http://www.computational.unibas.ch/cs/scicomp/software/pardiso/

INSTALLATION & USAGE::
====================

- Use a recent version of MKL (I tested MKL8.1).

- Build tfem using:

    make all pardiso

  for a usual build + pardiso.

- Use like:

  program myprog

    ...

    use pardiso_m

    ...

    call solve_system_pardiso ( sysmatrix, rhsd, sol )

    ...

  end program myprog

- set the environment variable OMP_NUM_THREADS to the number of processors
  available for the solver.


Remarks:
=======

- For symmetric systems the routine create_sysmatrix_structure must be called 
  with symmetric=.true.
  Also the solver variable matrixtype must be set to 2 (symmetric 
  positive definite) or -2 (symmetric indefinite) before the solve call, for
  example:

    solver_options%matrixtype = -2

    call solve_system_pardiso ( sysmatrix, rhsd, sol, &
      solver_options=solver_options )

  The default value is matrixtype=11 (a real unsymmetric matrix).

- For symmetric and structurally symmetric systems the diagonal elements must
  be available in the matrix. Since the diagonal zero blocks of the contraint
  equations and the additional unknowns equation are not stored in the matrix
  in TFEM, the zero diagonal needs to be added explicitly. This is
  done by calling both the routines:

    create_sysmatrix_structure_constraint 
    build_system_constraint

  with the argument "diagonal=.true."

- Depending on the detection of small pivots, pardiso performes two steps of
  iterative refinement. The actual number of iterative refinement steps
  performed can be printed by setting solver_options%printlevel=4. 
  By setting solver_options%niter_ref thei maximum number of iterative 
  refinement steps can be controlled.

- some first speed/memory indications on a 2.2Ghz dual-core Athlon64 using
  PARDISO from MKL-8.1 with BLAS from ACML-3.1.0 (all 64bit).

                                            MA41     PARDISO(1p) PARDISO(2p)
  2D Stokes problem (100x100 elements): 4.3s/0.32GB  7.0s/0.27GB  5.4s/0.27GB
  2D Stokes problem (200x200 elements):  35s/1.5GB    42s/1.2GB    30s/1.2GB
  3D Stokes problem (17x8x17 elements):  93s/1.6GB    51s/0.9GB    33s/0.9GB

